The decisive result is now clear. The theorem is syntactically tied to the submitted constructor term and rejects both a body mutation and a false return mutation, but it never derives the loop from the generated `<k>` semantics. Removing the single loop-summary simplification makes functional correctness fail, and the candidate contains no bridge-free theorem connecting that summary to real execution. I’m documenting this as a task-answer axiom rather than alleging a mathematically false rule.
